[ChapterTitle "The Classical: 5.Nf3 O-O 6.Be2 e5"]
[DefaultTags "vs-classical"]

{# The Classical: 5.Nf3 O-O 6.Be2 e5

White develops the knight to f3 and the bishop to e2, castles, and keeps the pawn on d4 as long as possible. This is the main line of the whole opening, and the chapter with the highest drill weight: a prepared opponent plays it, and so does one who is only developing sensibly.

## The seventh move

After 7.O-O Black plays 7...Na6. The knight is going to c5, where it hits e4 and holds the queenside, and it does not stand in the way of anything: the bishop on c8 still sees the board and the c-pawn is free to go to c6. The famous move is 7...Nc6, and the reason it is not here is the reason the course exists: after 8.d5 Ne7 both sides race, White with c5 and Black with ...f5 and ...g5, and whoever knows the theory further usually wins. The knight on a6 keeps the same closed centre without the race. 7...exd4 and 7...Nbd7 are sound and named where they arise.

## What White does with the centre

The chapter is arranged by White's answer to the lever. Keeping the tension with 8.Be3 or 8.Re1 is the main path, and in both Black plays around d4: ...Ng4 to hit the bishop, or ...c6 and ...exd4 with a knight jump to g4 that wins a pawn if White is careless. Closing with d5, on move seven or move eight, hands Black the ...f5 plan, and the knight goes to c5 or d7 to make room for it. Trading on e5 takes the queens off, and Black plays the ending with the bishop on g7 and the d-file, a pawn structure that is level and a game that is easier for the side with the better bishop.

## Piece homes

The queen goes to e8 in the ...Ng4 lines, where it holds e5, sees the kingside and stays out of the bishop's way. The rook on f8 stays for ...f5 in the closed centre and goes to e8 in the open one. The knight on a6 goes to c5 and stays until White pays to move it.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Playing 7...Nc6 from memory and then not knowing move twelve. Taking on d4 after 8.Be3 and letting the bishop sit on the square it wants. Moving the king's knight to h5 while the bishop on e2 can take it for free. Playing ...f5 before the knight has left f6. And in the queenless ending, forgetting that a knight on d5 with a bishop on g5 behind it is a fork waiting to happen.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O
{White castles and keeps the tension. The pawn on d4 stays and Black chooses. [%weight 8]}
( 7. d5
{White closes the centre at once. The Petrosian system if the bishop goes to g5 next, and the same closed structure whatever comes. [%weight 6]}
7... a5
{Stop b4 before it starts. With the centre closed, White's plan is c5 after b4, and the pawn on a5 makes b4 cost a move. [%tags queenside-defence closed-centre]}
( 7... Na6!?
{Sound and it transposes once ...a5 has been played too. The pawn first, because it stops b4 before Nd2 comes.}
) ( 7... Nbd7!?
{Sound: 8.Bg5 h6 9.Bh4 and the knight on d7 blocks the bishop on c8. The knight goes to a6 in this course.}
) ( 7... Ne8?!
{8.Bg5 f6 9.Bh4 and the pawn on f6 is in the way of the ...f5 push it was meant to prepare.}
) 8. Bg5 {The pin, with the pawn on d5 already there: the Petrosian system. [%weight 6]} (
8. O-O {White castles and the centre is closed. [%weight 6]} 8... Na6
{The knight to a6 and c5. [%tags development]} ( 8... Nbd7!?
{Sound, and it blocks the bishop on c8 for the ...f5 plan. The knight to a6 keeps the bishop free.}
) ( 8... Ne8!?
{Sound and it starts the attack a move early: 9.Ne1 f5 10.Nd3 and White is ready. The knight on a6 first.}
) 9. Ne1
{The knight goes to e1 to let f3 hold e4 and to come to d3 against the knight on c5. [%weight 7]}
9... Nd7
{The knight steps aside for the f-pawn, and from d7 it holds c5 and e5. [%tags f5-break]}
( 9... Nh5?! {10.Bxh5 gxh5 and the kingside is broken.} ) ( 9... Ne8!?
{Sound and the same plan; d7 holds c5 for the other knight.} ) 10. Be3
{The bishop develops and looks at c5. [%weight 6]} ( 10. Nd3
{The knight to d3, hitting c5 and holding e5. [%weight 4]} 10... f5
{The break. Nothing behind it is loose with the centre closed. [%tags f5-break]} (
10... Ndc5!?
{Sound: 11.Nxc5 Nxc5 12.Be3 and the knights have been traded on White's terms, with the bishop hitting c5. The break first.}
) 11. a3 {The pawn prepares b4. [%weight 6]} 11... Nac5
{The knight to c5, hitting e4 and the knight on d3. [%tags development] [%plan ...Nxd3 when the knight is traded, ...Nf6 and ...f4 with the pawns rolling. The exchange on c5 gives Black the d-file and the bishop.] [%line 7.d5 a5 with 9.Ne1 and 10.Nd3]}
( 11... Nf6!?
{Sound: 12.b4 and the queenside push has started with the knight on a6 in the way.} ) )
10... f5
{The break. The f-pawn goes, and the centre is closed so nothing behind it is loose. [%tags f5-break]}
( 10... Nac5!? {Sound: 11.f3 and the pawn on f5 comes a move later. The break first.} ) (
10... Ndc5!?
{Sound: 11.Bxc5 Nxc5 and the knight from a6 has taken back. The break first, because the bishop on e3 was going to be hit by ...f4 anyway.}
) 11. f3 {White holds e4 with a pawn and takes g4 from the knights. [%weight 8]} 11... f4
{Close the kingside. The bishop on e3 is hit, and the pawn on f4 is the front of the pawn storm. [%tags f5-break kingside-attack]}
( 11... fxe4?! {12.fxe4 and the f-file is White's, with the bishop on e3 untouched.} ) (
11... Nf6!? {Sound and slower: 12.exf5 gxf5 and the centre is open on White's terms.} )
12. Bf2 {The bishop steps back. [%weight 9]} 12... g5
{The pawns roll. ...g5, ...h5 and ...g4, with the rook on f8 lifting to f6 and h6. [%tags kingside-attack] [%plan ...Nf6, ...Rf7, ...Bf8 and ...Rg7, then ...g4. The knight on a6 goes to c5 when White plays b3. Everything Black owns is in front of the king, and White's play on the queenside takes longer.] [%line 7.d5 a5, the ...f5 and ...g5 storm]}
( 12... Nf6!? {Sound: 13.Nd3 and the knight is going to e5. The pawn first.} ) (
12... Ndc5?!
{13.Nd3 Nxd3 14.Qxd3 and the knight has been traded for nothing while the attack waited.}
) ) 8... h6
{Ask the bishop. It goes to h4, and the pawn on h6 is useful later for ...g5. [%tags tactics]}
( 8... Na6!? {Sound: 9.Nd2 and the bishop stays on g5. The pawn first makes it choose.} )
( 8... Nbd7?!
{9.Nd2 and the knight on d7 blocks the bishop with the bishop on g5 still pinning.} )
9. Bh4 {The bishop keeps the pin. [%weight 9]} 9... Na6
{The knight to a6 and c5, out of the way of the bishop on c8. [%tags development]} (
9... g5?!
{10.Bg3 and the pawn on g5 has loosened the king for a bishop that is happy on g3.} ) (
9... Nbd7?! {10.Nd2 and the knight blocks the bishop on c8 and does not hit e4.} ) 10. Nd2
{The knight goes to d2 to hold e4 and prepare b4. [%weight 8]} 10... Qe8
{The queen off the pin and behind the e-pawn. The knight on f6 is free to move now.} (
10... Nc5!?
{Sound: 11.O-O Qe8 comes a move later anyway, and the pin on f6 holds a move longer. The queen first.}
) ( 10... Nh7?
{11.Bxd8 and the queen has gone: the bishop on h4 was pinning the knight to it, and the knight walked away.}
) 11. O-O {White castles. [%weight 8]} 11... Nh7
{The knight steps aside for the f-pawn, and the bishop on h4 now looks at nothing. [%tags f5-break] [%plan ...Bd7 and ...Nc5, then ...f5. If White plays f3 the knight on h7 goes to g5 and f4 by way of ...h5. The bishop on h4 is White's worst piece and the pawn on d5 has nothing to push against on the queenside once ...a5 has been played.] [%line 7.d5 a5 8.Bg5, the Petrosian]}
( 11... Nc5!?
{Sound, and it holds e4. The knight to h7 first starts the plan the position is about.} )
( 11... g5?! {12.Bg3 and the pawn has loosened the king with the bishop happy on g3.} ) )
( 7. dxe5
{The Exchange variation. White takes the queens off and hopes for a draw, and Black has to know the shape or drift into a worse ending. [%weight 5]}
7... dxe5 {Take back with the pawn, and the queens come off. [%tags e5-lever]} (
7... Nxe4?
{8.Nxe4 dxe5 9.Qxd8 Rxd8 and Black has two pawns for the knight, with the queens off and nothing to show for them.}
) 8. Qxd8
{The queens come off, and this is the Exchange variation: drawn out for White, and Black has to know the shape. [%weight 9]}
8... Rxd8 {The rook takes back and the d-file is Black's. [%tags endgame]} ( 8... Nxe4?
{9.Qxf8+ Bxf8 10.Nxe4 and Black has lost a rook and a knight for a pawn: the queen on d8 had to be taken back first.}
) 9. Bg5 {The bishop hits the knight that holds e5. [%weight 7]} ( 9. Nd5
{The knight jumps in before the bishop is out. [%weight 3]} 9... Nxd5
{Take, and ...c6 undermines the pawn that arrives. [%tags endgame]} ( 9... Nbd7?!
{10.Nxf6+ Nxf6 11.Bg5 and the knight on f6 is pinned to the rook with the bishop on g7 the only piece that can break it: the capture first, then ...c6.}
) 10. cxd5 {The pawn takes back. [%weight 9]} 10... c6
{Hit the wedge. [%tags endgame] [%plan ...cxd5 and ...Nd7, ...Nc5 and ...Be6. The wedge comes off and the ending is level.] [%line 7.dxe5, the Exchange with 9.Nd5]}
( 10... f5?! {11.Bg5 and the bishop hits the rook with the f-pawn having weakened e6.} ) )
9... Re8 {The rook off the pin and holding e5. The knight is free. [%tags endgame]} (
9... Nbd7!?
{Sound: 10.Nd5 and the knight has to be answered with ...c6, which is fine. The rook first keeps the d-file.}
) ( 9... Rd6?! {10.Nd5 and the rook is hit.} ) 10. Nd5
{The knight jumps in with the bishop behind it. [%weight 8]} 10... Nxd5
{Take, because the pawn that comes to d5 can be undermined by ...c6, and there is no fork left. [%tags endgame]}
( 10... Nbd7?
{11.Nxc7 and the knight forks the rooks: 11...Rb8 12.Nxe8 and the exchange has gone. The knight on d5 has to be taken before it reaches c7.}
) ( 10... Nxe4?
{11.Nxc7 and the knight forks the rooks: 11...Nxg5 12.Nxg5 Re7 13.Nxa8 and the rook has gone. In the castled line the same capture is safe, because there the knight on a6 covers c7 and there is no fork.}
) 11. cxd5 {The pawn takes back and the wedge is on d5. [%weight 9]} 11... c6
{Hit the wedge at once. It comes off or it is held by pieces that would rather be elsewhere. [%tags endgame]}
( 11... Nd7?!
{12.O-O-O and White has the file with the wedge kept and the knight on d7 in the way of the rook: the pawn on c6 hits the wedge first.}
) ( 11... f6?! {12.Be3 and the bishop on g7 is blocked by its own pawn.} ) 12. Bc4
{The bishop holds the pawn. [%weight 8]} 12... cxd5
{Take, and the bishop takes back onto a square the knight on b8 will hit. [%tags endgame]}
( 12... b5?! {13.Bb3 and the pawn on b5 is a target while the wedge is still there.} )
13. Bxd5 {The bishop takes back. [%weight 9]} 13... Nd7
{The knight to d7, going to c5 and b6 against the bishop, or to f6 against it. The ending is level and Black's bishop is the one with a diagonal. [%tags endgame] [%plan ...Nc5 and ...Be6 to trade the bishop on d5, ...Rac8 and ...f6 when the bishop on g5 has to be asked. Level, and easier for Black, whose bishop has the long diagonal.] [%line 7.dxe5, the Exchange with 9.Bg5]}
( 13... Nc6!? {Sound: 14.O-O-O Nb4 15.Bb3 and the knight has spent two moves for a tempo.}
) ( 13... h6?! {14.Be3 and the bishop has been asked to go to a better square.} ) ) (
7. Be3
{The bishop first, before castling, so that ...Ng4 can be met by Bg5 with the king still on e1. [%weight 5]}
7... Ng4
{Hit the bishop at once. 7.Be3 with the king still on e1 invites it: the bishop goes to g5 and ...f6 pushes it again, with the king in the middle for a move or two longer than White wanted. [%tags tactics vs-classical]}
( 7... exd4!?
{Sound and the main theory: 8.Nxd4 Re8 9.f3 c6 10.Qd2 d5 with a sharp centre where every move matters. The knight jump keeps it simpler.}
) ( 7... Nc6!? {Sound: 8.d5 Ne7 and the race, with the bishop on e3 well placed for it.} )
( 7... Nbd7?!
{8.O-O and White has the position of the main line with the bishop already on e3 and no question asked.}
) 8. Bg5 {The bishop goes to g5, hitting the queen's diagonal. [%weight 9]} 8... f6
{Hit it again. The pawn on f6 is on its way to f5 in any case, and the bishop has no good square. [%tags f5-break]}
( 8... Qe8!?
{Sound: 9.O-O and it is the main line by another order. ...f6 first makes the bishop move now.}
) ( 8... h6?
{9.Bxd8 and the queen has gone: the bishop on g5 sees d8 through the square the knight left. The pawn on f6 hits the bishop without opening that diagonal.}
) 9. Bh4 {The bishop stays on the diagonal. [%weight 6]} ( 9. Bc1
{The bishop goes home, and White has spent two moves on it. [%weight 4]} 9... Nc6
{The knight hits d4, and the centre has to be decided while the bishop on c1 has done nothing. [%tags development]}
( 9... exd4!?
{Sound: 10.Nxd4 f5 and the open centre. The knight first makes White close it.} ) (
9... f5?! {10.exf5 Bxf5 11.h3 and the knight on g4 has to go back with the centre opened.}
