{Hit the bishop with the pawn. It has to go home to c1, because h4 walks into ...g5, and the knight on d5 is met by ...c6 next with the queen on e8 covering e7. [%tags tactics] [%plan ...c6 next to push the knight out, then ...Qe7, ...Rd8 and ...Nc5. The knight on d5 looks strong and has no support, and the bishop on g5 has been sent home.] [%line 8.Be3 Ng4 with 11.Nd5]}
( 11... c6?!
{12.Ne7+ Kh8 13.Nxc8 Rxc8 14.Qd6 and the knight has taken the bishop with the queen landing on d6: the pawn on f6 first, so that e7 is covered.}
) ( 11... Qd7?!
{12.c5 Nxc5 13.Qc2 and the knight on c5 is hit with the queen on d7 in the way of the bishop.}
) ( 11... Kh8?!
{12.h3 f6 13.Bc1 Nh6 14.c5 and the knight on g4 has gone to the rim with the c-pawn arriving.}
) ) 11... h6
{Ask the bishop first. It has to move, and whichever square it picks, the knight on g4 comes back to f6 with a tempo already spent by White. [%tags tactics]}
( 11... Nf6?!
{12.Bxf6 Bxf6 and the bishop on g7 has left the diagonal, which the setup never allows without a reason.}
) ( 11... Nh6?
{12.Qd2 and the knight on h6 is hit by the bishop with the queen behind it: 12...Ng4 13.hxg4 Bxg4 and a piece has gone for a pawn.}
) 12. Bd2
{The bishop retreats to d2, keeping an eye on a5 and the knight on c3. [%weight 7]} (
12. Bh4 {The bishop stays on the diagonal towards the queen. [%weight 3]} 12... Nf6
{The knight comes home, and the bishop on h4 is now the one out of play: it pins nothing, because the queen has left d8. [%tags development] [%plan ...Qe7 and ...Rd8, ...b6 and ...Nd7 to c5, and ...Nh5 when the bishop on h4 has nothing to do. The bishop on h4 is White's worst piece, so the game is about keeping it that way.] [%line The main line with 12.Bh4]}
( 12... g5?!
{13.Bg3 and the pawn on g5 has loosened the king for a bishop that is happy on g3, looking at e5.}
) ( 12... Qe7? {13.hxg4 and the knight has gone: the pawn on h3 was attacking it.} ) )
12... Nf6
{The knight comes home with the bishop on g7 clear, the queen on e8 and a pawn on e5. Every piece is where the setup wants it, and White has spent two moves on the bishop for nothing. [%tags development vs-classical] [%plan ...b6 and ...Nd7 to bring the knight to c5, ...Qe7 with a rook to d8, ...Nh5 and ...Nf4 when White's bishop returns to e3. The pawn on e5 is held by the queen and the bishop stays on g7; White's c-pawn has nothing to push against.] [%line The main line: 8.Be3 Ng4 9.Bg5 Qe8]}
( 12... Qe7?
{13.hxg4 and the knight has gone. The pawn on h3 was attacking it, and any move that is not the knight's loses it.}
) ( 12... f5?
{13.hxg4 and the knight has gone: the push did not save the piece the pawn was attacking.}
) *


