{The break, with the knight on f4 in front of it and the queen on e8 behind. The pawn on e4 is hit and the knight on f4 is held by the pawn on e5. [%tags f5-break] [%plan ...Nc5 and ...a5, ...fxe4 when Nxe4 can be met, ...Bd7 and ...Qe7. The knight on f4 is the best piece on the board and White has to spend g3 on it, which weakens f3 and h3 both.] [%line 6.h3: the knight to f4]}
( 11... Nc5!?
{Sound: 12.Bxc5 dxc5 13.h4 and the pawn on c5 is doubled with the break still to come.} )
( 11... Qe7!? {Sound: 12.a3 f5 and the same a move later.} ) ( 11... g5?!
{12.g3 and the knight on f4 has to go back with the kingside opened.} ) ) ( 5. Bd3
{The bishop to d3 before the knight comes out, so that the knight can go to e2 and the f-pawn is free. [%weight 2]}
5... O-O {Castle. [%tags king-safety]} ( 5... Nc6!?
{Sound: 6.Nge2 O-O and the same. The king first.} ) ( 5... e5!?
{Sound: 6.d5 O-O 7.Nge2 and the same shape with the centre closed a move earlier.} )
6. Nge2 {The knight to e2, behind the bishop. [%weight 8]} 6... Nc6
{The knight to c6, hitting d4. With the knight on e2 and the bishop on d3, d5 is met by ...Nd4 and the knight on d4 sits on the knight on e2's only square to trade it. [%tags development tactics]}
( 6... e5!?
{Sound: 7.d5 Nh5 8.Be3 Qh4 and the queen is out. The knight first hits d4 with a piece.} )
( 6... c5!? {Sound: 7.d5 e6 and a Benoni. The knight keeps the lever.} ) ( 6... Na6!?
{Sound: 7.h3 e5 8.d5 Nd7 and the same shapes. The knight on c6 asks about d4 now.} )
7. O-O {White castles. [%weight 9]} 7... e5
{The lever, with the knight on c6 behind it and d4 hit twice. [%tags e5-lever]} (
7... Nd7?! {8.Be3 e5 9.d5 and the knight on d7 blocks the bishop with the centre closed.}
) ( 7... Bd7?! {8.Be3 and the bishop has spent a move on nothing with d4 held.} ) 8. d5
{White closes. [%weight 8]} 8... Nd4
{The knight to d4. The knight on e2 takes or the knight stays, and if it takes the pawn on d4 is a wedge the knight on e2 has to spend a move on. [%tags tactics]}
( 8... Ne7?!
{9.f3 and the knight on e7 is in the way of the queen with White's storm ready.} ) (
8... Nb4?! {9.Bb1 and the knight has nothing to hit.} ) ( 8... Nb8?!
{Two moves lost: 9.Bg5 and White is ahead everywhere, though nothing has been lost yet.} )
9. Nxd4 {The knight takes. [%weight 8]} 9... exd4
{Take back with the pawn, which sits on d4 hitting the knight on c3. [%tags tactics]} (
9... Nxe4?
{10.Bxe4 and the knight has gone for a pawn: the pawn on e5 blocks the bishop on g7, so the knight on d4 is safe.}
) 10. Ne2 {The knight steps back, hitting the pawn. [%weight 8]} 10... Re8
{The rook to e8, hitting e4 through the knight. [%tags open-centre]} ( 10... c5!?
{Sound: 11.dxc6 bxc6 and the pawn is held with the b-file open. The rook first.} ) (
10... Nxe4? {11.Bxe4 and a knight has gone for a pawn.} ) 11. Bg5
{The bishop pins the knight, which holds the pawn on d4. [%weight 7]} 11... h6
{Ask the bishop. It takes or goes back, and the pawn on d4 is held by ...c5 next in either case. [%tags tactics]}
( 11... c5!?
{Sound: 12.dxc6 bxc6 13.Bxf6 Qxf6 and the pawn on d4 is held by the queen. The pawn on h6 first.}
) ( 11... Nxe4? {12.Bxe4 and a knight has gone for a pawn.} ) 12. Bxf6
{The bishop takes the knight. [%weight 8]} 12... Qxf6
{Take back with the queen, which holds d4 from f6 and sees the kingside. The bishop on g7 is kept and the pawn on d4 is held. [%tags tactics] [%plan ...c5 to fix the pawn on d4, ...Bd7 and ...Re7 with ...Rae8 against e4, ...Qe5 when the queen wants the centre. The pawn on d4 is a wedge and White's knight on e2 is stuck holding it.] [%line 5.Bd3 and 6.Nge2]}
( 12... Bxf6?!
{13.f4 and the pawn on d4 is held only by the bishop, which f5 will chase; the queen recapture holds d4 and keeps the bishop on g7.}
) ) 5... O-O
{Castle first. The centre is going to open, and the king wants to be out of the middle before it does. [%tags king-safety]}
( 5... c5!?
{Sound and it transposes after 6.Nf3 O-O. The king first, because 6.d5 O-O 7.Nf3 is the same and 6.dxc5 Qa5 wants the king safe.}
) ( 5... e5?!
{6.dxe5 dxe5 7.Qxd8+ Kxd8 8.fxe5 and a pawn has gone with the king in the middle: the pawn on f4 was placed to meet ...e5.}
) ( 5... Nbd7?!
{6.Nf3 e5 7.fxe5 dxe5 8.d5 and White has the centre with the knight on d7 in the way of everything.}
) 6. Nf3 {The knight to f3, holding d4 and e5. [%weight 9]} 6... c5
{Hit d4 from the side. The pawn on f4 was placed to meet ...e5, and ...c5 asks a question it does not answer: d5 gives a Benoni where the pawn on f4 is a target, dxc5 gives the pawn back at once. [%tags c5-break]}
( 6... e5?!
{7.fxe5 dxe5 8.d5 and White has the centre with a pawn on d5 and the e-file for the rook: the move the pawn on f4 was placed to meet.}
) ( 6... Na6!? {Sound: 7.Be2 c5 and the same a move later. The pawn first.} ) ( 6... Bg4?!
{7.Be2 and the bishop has left c8 for a pin that h3 breaks at once.} ) 7. d5
{White pushes, and it is a Benoni with a pawn on f4. [%weight 7]} ( 7. dxc5
{White takes, and the pawn comes back with the queen. [%weight 2]} 7... Qa5
{The queen to a5, pinning the knight on c3 and taking c5 back next move. The pawn on e4 is loose the moment the knight on c3 moves. [%tags tactics]}
( 7... dxc5?!
{8.Qxd8 Rxd8 9.e5 and the knight on f6 has to move with White's centre rolling.} ) (
7... Nc6!?
{Sound: 8.cxd6 exd6 9.Be2 and Black has the open lines for the pawn. The queen move takes the pawn back.}
) 8. Bd3 {The bishop holds e4 so that the knight on c3 can move. [%weight 8]} 8... Qxc5
{Take the pawn back. The queen on c5 is not loose: nothing hits it yet, and it holds the knight on f6 and sees the kingside. [%tags tactics]}
( 8... Nc6?! {9.cxd6 exd6 10.O-O and a pawn has gone.} ) ( 8... dxc5?!
{9.O-O Nc6 10.Qe1 and the pawn on c5 is a weakness with the queen on a5 out of things.} )
9. Qe2 {The queen holds e4 and the bishop. [%weight 8]} 9... Bg4
{The bishop to g4, pinning the knight to the queen. The knight on f3 is the piece holding e5 and d4, and the pin makes it hard to move. [%tags tactics]}
( 9... Nc6!? {Sound: 10.Be3 Qh5 11.h3 and the queen has found h5. The bishop first pins.}
) ( 9... Ng4?!
{10.Be3 and the knight on g4 is hit with the bishop hitting the queen as well.} ) 10. Be3
{The bishop hits the queen. [%weight 9]} 10... Qa5
{The queen back to a5, where it pins the knight on c3 again and holds the queenside. The bishop on e3 has hit it and gained nothing. [%tags queenside-defence]}
( 10... Qh5?!
{11.h3 Bxf3 12.Qxf3 Qxf3 13.gxf3 and the queens have come off on White's terms with the bishop pair White's.}
) ( 10... Qc7!? {Sound: 11.O-O Nc6 12.h3 Bxf3 and the same. On a5 the queen pins.} )
11. O-O {White castles. [%weight 9]} 11... Nc6
{The knight to c6, holding d4 and looking at b4. Every piece is out and the pawn is back; the pin on f3 and the pin on c3 are Black's to keep. [%plan ...Rac8 and ...Nb4 against the bishop on d3, ...Bxf3 when h3 asks, ...Nd7 and ...Nc5. The centre has opened with every Black piece out and the queen on a5 pinning c3.] [%line The Four Pawns with 7.dxc5]}
( 11... Nbd7!?
{Sound: 12.h3 Bxf3 13.Qxf3 and the knight on d7 is going to c5. The knight on c6 holds d4 and b4 both.}
) ( 11... Bxf3!?
{Sound: 12.Qxf3 Nc6 and the same a move later. The knight first, so that Bxf3 comes when h3 asks it to.}
) ) ( 7. Be2 {The bishop develops and keeps the tension. [%weight 2]} 7... cxd4
{Take, and the knight recaptures onto a square the knight on c6 will hit. [%tags c5-break]}
( 7... Nc6!? {Sound: 8.d5 Na5 9.Bd3 and the knight on a5 hits c4. The take first.} ) (
7... Qb6?!
{8.d5 and the queen on b6 is hit next by Na4 with the centre closed on White's terms.} )
8. Nxd4 {The knight takes back. [%weight 9]} 8... Nc6
{The knight hits d4, and the knight on d4 has to be held or traded.} ( 8... Qb6?!
{9.Nb3 and the queen has been hit with nothing gained.} ) ( 8... e5?!
{9.Nb3 exf4 10.Bxf4 and the pawn on d6 is a target with the knight on b3 looking at it.} )
9. Be3 {The bishop holds the knight. [%weight 8]} 9... Bg4
{The bishop to g4, hitting the bishop on e2, and the trades that follow are forced: 10.Nxc6 Bxe2 11.Nxd8 Bxd1 12.Rxd1 Rfxd8, and it is level with the queens off and Black's rook already on the d-file. [%tags tactics]}
( 9... Ng4!?
{Sound and sharper: 10.Bxg4 Bxd4 11.Bxd4 Bxg4 12.Qxg4 Nxd4 and it is level after a longer dance. The bishop to g4 needs less counting.}
) ( 9... e5?!
{10.Nxc6 bxc6 11.fxe5 dxe5 12.Qxd8 Rxd8 13.Bc5 and the pawns on c6 and e5 are targets.} )
10. Nxc6 {The knight takes, and the sequence begins. [%weight 8]} 10... Bxe2
{Take the bishop, hitting the queen. The knight on c6 is hanging too, and White has to take the queen first. [%tags tactics]}
( 10... bxc6?
{11.Bxg4 Rb8 12.Bf3 and the bishop on g4 has simply been taken, with the pawn on c6 doubled: the sequence had to be played in order, the bishop taking on e2 before anything else.}
) 11. Nxd8 {The knight takes the queen. [%weight 9]} 11... Bxd1
{Take the queen back. [%tags tactics]} ( 11... Rfxd8?
{12.Qxe2 and the bishop has gone: the queen on d1 was attacked and had to be taken first.}
) 12. Rxd1 {The rook takes the bishop. [%weight 9]} 12... Rfxd8
{Take the knight, and count: piece for piece and the queens off, with Black's rook on d8 hitting d6 and White's king on e1. [%tags endgame] [%plan ...Nd7 and ...Nc5 or ...Ne5, ...Rac8 and ...a6. Level, with the bishop on g7 the best piece on the board and White's king still to castle.] [%line The Four Pawns with 7.Be2]}
( 12... Raxd8!? {Sound and the other rook: the f-rook is the one that was doing nothing.}
) ) 7... e6
{The break at once. The pawn on d5 is hit before White has castled, and the e-file opens for the rook. [%tags e5-lever]}
( 7... b5!?
{Sound and sharp: 8.cxb5 a6 and a Benko shape with the pawn on f4 loose. More to remember.}
) ( 7... Nbd7?!
{8.Be2 and the knight on d7 blocks the bishop on c8 with the break still to come.} ) (
7... Qa5?! {8.Bd2 and the queen on a5 is hit next by Nb5 with nothing gained.} ) 8. Be2
{The bishop develops, and the pawn on d5 is held by the pawn on c4. [%weight 8]} 8... exd5
{Take, so that the recapture decides the structure: cxd5 gives Black the e-file and the c-pawn, exd5 gives the e-file and a bishop on f5. [%tags open-centre]}
( 8... Re8!? {Sound: 9.O-O exd5 10.cxd5 and the same a move later.} ) ( 8... Na6?!
{9.dxe6 Bxe6 10.f5 and the bishop is hit with the centre broken on White's terms.} )
9. cxd5 {The pawn takes back with the c-file half open. [%weight 7]} ( 9. exd5
{The pawn takes back from e4, and the e-file is open for both sides. [%weight 3]} 9... Bf5
{The bishop to f5, where nothing hits it: the pawn on e4 has gone and the bishop on e2 does not see it. The knight on f6 goes to e4 next.}
( 9... Re8!?
{Sound: 10.O-O Na6 and the same shape a move later. The bishop first, because e4 is the square.}
) ( 9... Bg4!?
{Sound: 10.O-O Nbd7 and the trade on f3 comes. With the pawn on d5 rather than e4, the bishop on f5 is the better square.}
) 10. O-O {White castles. [%weight 9]} 10... Ne4
{The knight to e4, in the middle of the board with the bishop on f5 behind it. White trades it or lives with it. [%tags development tactics]}
( 10... Re8!?
{Sound: 11.Bd3 Bxd3 12.Qxd3 and the bishops have come off. The knight first.} ) (
10... Nbd7?! {11.Nh4 and the bishop on f5 is hit with the knight on f6 not yet on e4.} )
11. Nxe4 {White trades the knight. [%weight 8]} 11... Bxe4
{Take back with the bishop, which sits on e4 hitting d5 and the queenside, and cannot be pushed: no pawn reaches e4 now. [%plan ...Nd7 and ...Nf6 against d5, ...Re8 and ...Qb6 or ...Qa5. The bishop on e4 is the best piece on the board and White has to spend a bishop on it.] [%line The Four Pawns with 9.exd5]}
( 11... Re8?
{12.Ng3 and the knight has gone for nothing: it was hanging on e4 and the rook on e8 does not take it back.}
) ) 9... Bg4
{The bishop to g4, to trade the knight on f3. That knight holds e5 and d4, and without it the pawn on e4 is loose and the knight on f6 has e4 and g4 to jump to. The one line where Black gives up the bishop on c8 on purpose. [%tags tactics]}
( 9... Re8!?
{Sound: 10.O-O Bg4 and the same. The bishop first, because Nd2 would keep the knight.} ) (
9... Na6?! {10.O-O Nc7 11.a4 and the knight has spent two moves getting to c7.} ) (
9... Nxe4? {10.Nxe4 and the knight has gone for a pawn: e4 was held by the knight on c3.}
) 10. O-O {White castles. [%weight 9]} 10... Nbd7
{The knight to d7, going to b6 against d5 or to e5 when the knight on f3 has been traded.}
( 10... Na6!?
{Sound: 11.h3 Bxf3 12.Bxf3 Nc7 and the knight is on c7. The knight to d7 keeps b6 and e5 both.}
) ( 10... Bxf3!?
{Sound: 11.Bxf3 Nbd7 and the same. The knight first, so that the bishop takes when h3 asks it to.}
) 11. h3 {The pawn asks the bishop. [%weight 8]} 11... Bxf3
{Take, and the knight on f3 has gone: e4 is held by the knight on c3 alone, and e5 is Black's. [%tags tactics]}
( 11... Bh5?!
{12.g4 Bxg4 13.hxg4 Nxg4 and Black has two pawns for the piece with the bishop on e2 still on the board: not enough.}
) ( 11... Be6?
{12.dxe6 fxe6 13.Qxd6 and two pawns have gone: the bishop walked onto the square the pawn on d5 was attacking.}
) 12. Bxf3 {The bishop takes back. [%weight 9]} 12... Re8
{The rook to e8, hitting e4 through the knight on f6. The pawn is held by the knight on c3 and the bishop on f3, and ...c4 with ...Nc5 comes next to hit it again. [%tags open-centre] [%plan ...c4 and ...Nc5 against e4, ...Rc8 and ...Qb6 or ...a6 with ...b5. The knight on f3 has gone, so e5 is a square for Black's knights and the pawn on e4 is the target.] [%line The Four Pawns: 7.d5 e6]}
( 12... Ne8!?
{Sound: 13.a4 Nc7 14.Be3 and the knight is on c7 with ...b5 coming. The rook first.} ) (
12... Nxe4? {13.Nxe4 and the knight has gone for a pawn: the rook was not on e8 yet.} ) (
12... c4!? {Sound: 13.Be3 Nc5 14.Bxc5 dxc5 and the pawn on c4 is held. The rook first.} )
