{10.Bh2 Nxc3 11.bxc3 and the bishop has escaped: it was there to be taken, and the knight has been traded instead.}
) ) 9... dxe5
{Take back with the pawn. The knight on d7 holds e5, the queens may come off, and the d-file is Black's. Not 9...Nxe5, which lets the knight on f3 trade and the bishop on f4 take with the queens off. [%tags e5-lever vs-london]}
( 9... Nxe5?!
{10.Nxe5 dxe5 11.Qxd8 Rxd8 12.Bxe5 and the pawn has gone with the queens off: the knight took what the pawn should have.}
) ( 9... Ne4!?
{Sound: 10.Qc2 dxe5 11.Bh2 and the same shape with the knight already on e4. The pawn first keeps the knight's choice.}
) ( 9... Nh5?!
{10.Bg5 f6 11.exf6 and the pawn on f6 has to be taken back with the kingside loosened.} )
10. Bh2
{The bishop steps back to h2, which is what h3 was for: the London course's move, and the pawn on e5 is now a target for the knight on f3. [%weight 5]}
( 10. Nxe5
{The knight takes the pawn, and the engine likes it: the queens come off next and the bishop on f4 takes back on e5. The answer is a knight move that does not take. [%weight 5]}
10... Nh5
{The knight to h5, hitting the bishop on f4. Not 10...Nxe5, which loses a pawn to the queen trade and the bishop taking back. The bishop on f4 is hit, the knight on e5 is hit by the knight on d7, and White has to give one of them up: 11.Bxh5 Nxe5 and the knight on e5 comes to d3 next. [%tags tactics vs-london]}
( 10... Nxe5?
{11.Qxd8 Rxd8 12.Bxe5 and the pawn has gone: the queen trade came first, so the knight on e5 was never taken back.}
) ( 10... Ne4?! {11.Nd3 and the knight on e5 has gone home with the pawn kept.} ) (
10... c6?! {11.Nxd7 Nxd7 12.Nc3 and the pawn has gone with nothing for it.} ) 11. Bxh5
{The bishop takes the knight, and the pawn on e5 falls back to the knight on d7. [%weight 8]}
11... Nxe5
{Take the knight. The pawn is level and the knight on e5 is going to d3, where it forks nothing but sits on a square White cannot cover. [%tags tactics]}
( 11... gxh5?! {12.Nxd7 Qxd7 13.Bxc7 and the pawns have gone: the knight first.} ) (
11... Bxe5?!
{12.Bxe5 Nxe5 13.Bxg6? No: 12.Bxe5 Nxe5 13.Be2 and the bishop on g7 has gone for the bishop on f4.}
) 12. Be2 {The bishop steps back. [%weight 8]} 12... Nd3
{The knight to d3, hitting the bishop on f4 and the pawn on b2, on a square nothing can hold. White gives the queen for the knight and the queen: 13.Qxd3 Qxd3 14.Bxd3, and then the bishop on g7 takes on b2 and the pawn is back. [%tags tactics]}
( 12... Be6!?
{Sound: 13.Qb3 c6 14.Rd1 and the pawn is not back yet, but the pieces are out. The knight to d3 wins it back at once.}
) ( 12... Nxc4?
{13.Bxc4 Qxd1 14.Rxd1 Bxb2 15.Nd2 Bxa1 16.Rxa1 and White has a knight and a bishop against a rook, with the pawn still White's: the knight to d3 gets the pawn back cleanly.}
) ( 12... f5?!
{13.Nc3 and the knight on e5 has to decide with the kingside loosened for nothing.} )
13. Qxd3
{The queen takes the knight, so that the bishop takes back after the trade. [%weight 8]} (
13. Bxd3
{The bishop takes the knight, and the queen on d1 is hit by nothing yet. [%weight 2]}
13... Bxb2
{Take on b2, hitting the rook on a1. Not 13...Qxd3, which walks into 14.Qxd3: the queen on d1 sees d3 the moment the pawn on d4 has gone. [%tags tactics] [%plan ...Bxa3 after Na3, ...Qe7 and ...Be6, ...Rad8. The pawn is back and the rook on a1 has been hit on the way.] [%line The London with 10.Nxe5 and 13.Bxd3]}
( 13... Qxd3?
{14.Qxd3 and the queen has gone: the queen on d1 was looking straight at d3.} ) (
13... g5? {14.Bxh7+ Kxh7 15.Qh5+ Kg8 16.Bxg5 and the king has been opened for a pawn.} ) (
13... Bxh3? {14.gxh3 and the bishop has gone for a pawn.} ) ) 13... Qxd3
{Take the queen. [%tags tactics]} ( 13... Bxb2?
{14.Qxd8 Rxd8 15.Nd2 and a knight has gone for a pawn: the queen on d3 had to be taken first.}
) 14. Bxd3 {The bishop takes back. [%weight 9]} 14... Bxb2
{Take on b2. The pawn is back, the rook on a1 is hit, and the knight on b1 has to come out to a3 to hold everything. [%tags tactics] [%plan ...Bxa3 when Na3 comes, or ...Be5 with the bishop kept, ...Be6 hitting c4, ...Rad8 for the file. Level, and Black's pieces are the active ones.] [%line The London with 10.Nxe5 and ...Nh5]}
( 14... Be6?
{15.Nc3 and the pawn on b2 is held for good: White is a pawn up with the pieces out. The bishop takes it while it is free.}
) ( 14... Bxh3? {15.gxh3 and the bishop has gone for a pawn.} ) ) 10... Ne4
{The knight to e4, in the middle of the board with the pawn on e5 behind it. From e4 it hits nothing yet and cannot be pushed: no pawn reaches e4 with c4 and e3 played, and the bishop on e2 does not see it. [%tags development vs-london]}
( 10... Qe7!?
{Sound: 11.Nc3 a5 12.Qc2 c6 and a quiet game with White a little better. The knight to e4 asks a question.}
) ( 10... e4?! {11.Nd4 and the knight is on d4 with the pawn on e4 a target for f3.} ) (
10... c6!? {Sound: 11.Nc3 Qe7 12.Qc2 and the same. The knight first.} ) 11. Nbd2
{The knight offers a trade. [%weight 6]} ( 11. Qc2
{The queen hits the knight. [%weight 4]} 11... f5
{Hold the knight with the pawn. The kingside is safe with the bishop on g7, and the pawn on f5 is part of the ...e4 push to come. [%tags f5-break]}
( 11... Ndf6!?
{Sound: 12.Nbd2 Nxd2 13.Qxd2 and the same shape with the knight on f6. The pawn holds e4 and keeps the knight there.}
) ( 11... Nef6?! {12.Nc3 and the knight has gone back with nothing to show for the trip.}
) 12. Nbd2 {The knight offers a trade. [%weight 8]} 12... Nxd2 {Take.} ( 12... Ndf6?!
{13.Nxe4 fxe4 14.Nd2 and the pawn on e4 is held by the queen alone with the knight on d2 hitting it.}
) 13. Qxd2 {The queen takes back. [%weight 9]} 13... Qe7
{The queen to e7, holding e5 and the queenside, and connecting the rooks. Level, with the pawn on f5 ready to go to f4 when the bishop on h2 has nothing to do. [%plan ...a5 and ...Nc5, ...c6 and ...e4 when the knight on f3 has to move, ...f4 when the bishop on h2 is shut in by its own pawn on e3. Level, and Black's pieces are the ones with somewhere to go.] [%line The London with 10.Bh2 and 11.Qc2]}
( 13... e4?!
{14.Nd4 and the knight is on d4 with the pawn on e4 held by the pawn on f5 and nothing else.}
) ( 13... a5!?
{Sound: 14.c5 Nxc5 15.Qxd8 Rxd8 16.Bxe5 and the queens have come off. The queen move keeps them on.}
) ) 11... Nxd2
{Take, and the queen comes to d2: the knight on d7 goes to c5 or f6 next with nothing on d2 to hit it.}
( 11... f5?
{12.Nxe4 fxe4 13.Nd2 and the pawn on e4 is hit by the knight and held by nothing: it goes, and the trade first keeps the structure.}
) ( 11... Ndf6?!
{12.Nxe4 Nxe4 13.Qc2 and the knight on e4 is hit with the queen on c2 hitting nothing else.}
) 12. Qxd2 {The queen takes back. [%weight 9]} 12... a5
{The pawn to a5, taking b4 from White and holding c5 for the knight. The queenside is fixed before White's c5 arrives, and ...Nc5 next hits nothing but stands well. [%tags queenside-defence] [%plan ...Nc5 or ...Nf6, ...Qe7 and ...c6, ...e4 only when the knight on f3 has moved. The bishop on h2 is looking at a pawn that is held three times and the position is level.] [%line The London: 8.c4 e5 9.dxe5, 10.Bh2]}
( 12... Qe7!?
{Sound: 13.b4 a5 14.a3 and the same shape with b4 played. The pawn on a5 first, because c5 was coming.}
) ( 12... c6!?
{Sound: 13.b4 a5 14.a3 and the queenside has been fixed by White. The pawn on a5 first.} )
( 12... e4?! {13.Nd4 and the knight is on d4 with the pawn on e4 a target for f3.} ) *


[CourseId "kings-indian-black"]
[CourseTitle "The King's Indian Defence"]
[StudentColour "black"]
[Tagline "Concede the centre, then hit it: one setup against 1.d4, 1.c4 and 1.Nf3, as Black."]
[FormatVersion "1"]
[TagExtensions "e5-lever f5-break closed-centre open-centre dark-bishop c5-break vs-classical vs-samisch vs-fianchetto vs-london queenside-defence"]
[ChapterId "ch6-tactics-and-plans"]
[ChapterTitle "Typical tactics and plans"]
[DefaultTags "tactics"]

{# Typical tactics and plans

Eight positions, each one running through a line from an earlier chapter and stopping on the move that matters. Play the line and find the last move yourself.

## What each one is about

The first is the storm. The centre is closed, the f-pawn has gone to f4 and the g-pawn to g5, and the piece that decides the attack is the rook: it lifts to f6 and h6, and the queen comes to h5 behind it while White is taking a pawn on the other wing.

The second is the knight on f4. It got there by way of h5 because the knight on f3 was in the bishop's way, and when White plays g3 to push it, the pawn on h3 is the one it takes.

The third and fourth are the ...Nxe4 trick, twice: once after the queens have come off in the Classical, where a knight on d5 looks like a fork and is not one, and once in the h3 line, where White takes a pawn on e5 that the knight on f6 was only pretending to hold. In chapter 1 the same capture loses the exchange when White has not castled, so the third exercise is also about counting.

The fifth is the ...Qb6 trick: a knight on g4 that cannot be taken because the queen behind it hits d4 and b2 both, and the queen that lands on d4 afterwards has to be brought home without walking into the pawn on e4.

The sixth is the exchange of the dark bishop. The bishop on g7 is the defence and is never traded for nothing; here it goes to h6 to hit the knight on d2, and when the bishop on c1 has nowhere to go the trade is right, because the f-pawn goes next.

The seventh and eighth are defence against the queenside pawns. In the Petrosian, b4 arrives with the rook behind it and the answer is ...axb4 and a knight to a4, where taking it hands Black a bishop that looks at d1 and turns White's c5 break into a lost queen. In the other, White pushes b4 with a knight on c5 in the way, and the a-pawn takes because the pawn on b6 is holding the knight.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Lifting the rook before the f-pawn has closed the kingside. Retreating the knight from f4 when it can take on h3. Playing ...Nxe4 with the king still on e1 for White, when Nxc7 forks the rooks. Trading the bishop on g7 because a trade is available rather than because the c1 bishop is stuck. And answering b4 by retreating the knight, when the a-pawn takes and the knight goes forward.}
