11... Ne5!?
{Sound: 12.Nf3 Nxf3+ 13.Qxf3 and White has the bishop pair. The queen move keeps the pawn.}
) 12. hxg4 {White takes the knight. [%weight 8]} 12... Qxd4 {Take back on d4.} 13. Qe2
{The queen holds e4 and g4. [%weight 6]} 13... Qe5 {The queen to e5. [%tags king-safety]}
14. g3 {The pawn to g3, taking f4 from the queen and preparing Bf4. [%weight 7]} 14... Nc5
{The knight to c5, hitting e4 a second time. Bf4 is coming to hit the queen, and the knight is placed first so that the queen has e7 to go to with the knight covering d3 and e4. [%tags development]}
( 14... Qe7!?
{Sound: 15.Bf4 Be5 and the queen has left e5 for the bishop. The knight first hits e4.} )
( 14... Qxe4?
{15.Qxe4 and the queen has gone for a pawn: e4 was held by the queen, the rook and the knight.}
) 15. Bf4 {The bishop hits the queen. [%weight 8]} 15... Qe7
{Exercise 5, the last move: the queen to e7, home and safe. Not e8, where Bxd6 forks the queen and the knight, and not f6, where the queen is hit by Bxd6 and Nd4 both. The knight on c5 holds e4 and d3, and Black has a pawn for nothing with the queens likely to stay on. [%tags king-safety tactics] [%plan ...Be5 to trade the bishop on f4, ...Re8 and ...Bd7, ...Nxe4 when it is free. A pawn up with the kingside pawns on g4 and g3 loose.] [%line Exercise 5: the ...Qb6 trick]}
( 15... Qe8?
{16.Bxd6 and the bishop forks the queen and the knight on c5: the queen on e8 was the one square that lost a piece.}
) ( 15... Qf6?!
{16.Rad1 Ne6 17.Bxd6 and the pawn on d6 has gone with the queen on f6 hit next by Nd4.} )
( 15... Qe6?! {16.Rad1 Be5 17.Qe3 and the queen on e6 is in the way of the bishop on c8.}
) ) ( 8. d5 {White closes. [%weight 5]} 8... Nc5 {The knight to c5. [%tags development]}
9. Qc2 {The queen holds e4. [%weight 6]} ( 9. Nd2 {The knight to d2. [%weight 4]} 9... a5
{Hold the knight. [%tags queenside-defence]} 10. b3 {The pawn to b3. [%weight 6]}
10... Bh6 {The bishop to h6, hitting the knight on d2. [%tags dark-bishop]} 11. Qc2
{The queen holds the knight, and the bishop on c1 is pinned to the job of holding d2. [%weight 7]}
11... Ne8
{Exercise 6: the knight to e8. It steps aside for the f-pawn and covers d6, and the bishop on h6 stays: White cannot play Bb2 without losing d2, so the bishop on c1 has to sit. [%tags f5-break dark-bishop]}
( 11... Bxd2?!
{12.Bxd2 and the bishop on g7 has been traded for a knight, which is the one trade the setup never makes without a reason: the reason was coming, and it was the bishop on c1.}
) ( 11... Nh5?! {12.Bxh5 gxh5 13.Nf3 and the kingside is broken.} ) ( 11... Kh8!?
{Sound: 12.Bb2 Rg8 and the g-file is Black's. The knight to e8 keeps ...f5 as the plan.} )
12. Nf3 {The knight moves out of the pin. [%weight 7]} 12... Bxc1
{Take the bishop. The knight has left d2, so the bishop on h6 has nothing to hit there, and the bishop on c1 is the one White wanted on b2: it goes, and the f-pawn goes next. [%tags dark-bishop tactics]}
( 12... Bg7?!
{13.Bb2 and the bishop has gone back to g7 with White's bishop on b2 where it wanted to be: the trade was there and was not taken.}
) ( 12... f5?
{13.Bxh6 fxe4 14.Ng5 and the bishop on g7 has gone for nothing with the knight arriving on g5: the trade on c1 first.}
) 13. Raxc1 {The rook takes back. [%weight 9]} 13... f5
{Exercise 6, the last move: the break. With the dark bishops off, White's king has no bishop to hold the dark squares, and the pawn on f5 opens the f-file for the rook that is already there. [%tags f5-break] [%plan ...Nf6 and ...Qe7, ...fxe4 when Nxe4 lets the knight on c5 trade, ...Rf7 and ...Raf8. The dark squares around White's king are Black's.] [%line Exercise 6: the ...Bh6 exchange]}
( 13... Qe7!? {Sound: 14.a3 f5 and the same a move later.} ) ( 13... Ng7!?
{Sound: 14.a3 f5 and the same. The break first.} ) ( 13... Bd7?!
{14.a3 and the break has been delayed with the bishop on d7 doing nothing.} ) ) 9... a5
{Hold the knight. [%tags queenside-defence]} 10. Bg5 {The bishop to g5. [%weight 7]}
10... h6 {Ask the bishop.} 11. Be3 {The bishop to e3. [%weight 8]} 11... b6
{Hold the knight with the pawn. [%tags queenside-defence]} 12. Nd2
{The knight to d2. [%weight 7]} 12... Qe8 {The queen to e8. [%tags vs-classical]} 13. Nb3
{The knight to b3, to trade the knight on c5. [%weight 7]} 13... Nfd7
{Exercise 8: the knight to d7, holding c5 a second time so that Nxc5 is met by a knight and not a pawn, and stepping aside for the f-pawn. Two jobs in one move. [%tags queenside-defence f5-break]}
( 13... Nxb3?!
{14.Qxb3 and the queen is on b3 hitting b7 with the f-pawn still at home: the knight was worth keeping.}
) ( 13... Na6!?
{Sound: 14.Rae1 Bd7 and the knight has stepped back. The knight to d7 holds c5 and prepares ...f5 both.}
) ( 13... Ng4?! {14.Bd2 and the knight on g4 has nothing to do with h3 coming.} ) 14. Nxc5
{The knight trades. [%weight 7]} 14... Nxc5
{Take back with the knight, so that the pawn on b6 still holds it and the structure is untouched. [%tags queenside-defence]}
( 14... bxc5?!
{15.Nb5 and the knight is in with the pawn on a5 loose: the structure has been given away.}
) ( 14... dxc5?!
{15.Rad1 and the pawn on d6 has gone from the centre with the d-file White's.} ) 15. b4
{The storm: the pawn to b4, hitting the knight on c5 with the a-pawn behind it. [%weight 6]}
15... axb4
{Exercise 8, the last move: the a-pawn takes. The knight on c5 is held by the pawn on b6, so b4 threatened nothing it could carry out, and the pawn on b4 is now Black's with the a-file open behind it. The knight on c3 has to move and the queenside is Black's. [%tags queenside-defence tactics] [%plan ...Qe7 and ...f5, ...Ra3 when the knight on c3 has moved, ...Bd7 and ...Rfb8. The storm hit a knight that was held, and the pawn it pushed is Black's.] [%line Exercise 8: the storm meets the a-pawn]}
( 15... Na6?! {16.bxa5 and the pawn has gone with the knight on the rim.} ) ( 15... Nd7?!
{16.Nb5 and the knight is in on b5 with c5 coming: the knight on c5 left the square that was holding everything.}
) ( 15... Nb7?! {16.a3 and the knight on b7 is out of the game with the storm a move on.}
) ) 8... dxe5 {Take back. [%tags e5-lever]} 9. Qxd8 {The queens come off. [%weight 8]}
9... Rxd8 {Take back. [%tags endgame]} 10. Bg5 {The bishop hits the knight. [%weight 7]}
10... Re8 {The rook steps off the pin. [%tags endgame]} 11. Nd5
{The knight jumps in, and it looks like a fork. [%weight 6]} 11... Nxe4
{Exercise 3: take on e4. The fork on e7 wins nothing, because the knight on e7 is traded for the bishop on c8 and the pawn on e4 was free. The same capture loses when White has not castled, because Nxc7 hits both rooks; here the rook on a8 is covered by the knight on a6.}
( 11... Nd7?!
{12.Nc3 and the ending is level, with the pawn on e4 still on the board: the capture on e4 is the exercise.}
) ( 11... Nxd5?
{12.cxd5 Nb8 13.Rac1 and the pawn on d5 is a wedge with the bishop pair White's and the knight back on b8.}
) 12. Ne7+ {The fork. [%weight 9]} 12... Kf8 {The king steps up.} 13. Nxc8
{The knight takes the bishop. [%weight 9]} 13... Raxc8 {Take back. [%tags endgame]}
14. Be3 {The bishop steps back, and the pawn is gone for nothing. [%weight 7]} 14... Nb4
{Exercise 3, the last move: the knight to b4, hitting a2 and c2 and the square d3. The extra pawn is on the board and every Black piece has a job. [%tags tactics endgame] [%plan ...Nxa2 or ...Nc2 when it is free, ...f5 to hold the knight on e4, ...Ke7 and the rooks to the d-file. A pawn up with the better minor pieces.] [%line Exercise 3: the ...Nxe4 trick, castled]}
( 14... f5!? {Sound and the same count: 15.g3 Nb4 and the knight comes a move later.} ) (
14... b6!? {Sound: 15.Rad1 Nb4 and the same.} ) ( 14... Nac5!?
{Sound: 15.Bxc5+ Nxc5 and the knights have been traded on White's terms; the knight to b4 keeps both.}
